{"name":"Chicha Morada","hex":"#7e6072","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chicha","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fermented beverage from South and Central America, most commonly made from maize, grapes or apples."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A genre of popular Peruvian music related to cumbia and native Peruvian huayno."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chicha"},{"term":"morada","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["a religious meeting hall associated with the Penitente Brotherhood of New Mexico"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/morada"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chicha morada","blocks":[{"text":"Chicha morada is a beverage from the Andean regions of Perú that is currently consumed at an international level."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Chicha_morada_y_pisco_sour_20100704.JPG/330px-Chicha_morada_y_pisco_sour_20100704.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicha_morada"},{"title":"Chicha","blocks":[{"text":"Chicha is a fermented (alcoholic) or non-fermented beverage of Latin America, emerging from the Andes and Amazonia regions. In both the pre- and post-Spanish conquest periods, corn beer made from a variety of maize landraces has been the most common form of chicha. However, chicha is also made from a variety of other cultigens and wild plants, including, among others, quinoa, kañiwa, peanut, manioc, palm fruit, rice, potato, oca, and chañar. There are many regional variations of chicha. In the Inca Empire, chicha had ceremonial and ritual uses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg/330px-Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicha"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Chicha_mo